<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1310–1367</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Cardinal Albornoz, Gil de Albornoz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz was a Castilian archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and cardinal who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> popes as legate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. From 1353 he reconquered the Papal States from local lords and city tyrants by war and diplomacy, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egidian-constitutions">Egidian Constitutions</a> of 1357 gave them a lasting legal framework. He founded the Spanish College at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz.jpg" alt="Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz" width="222" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albornoz_detail,_Bonaiuto_fresco.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
